      "content": "That's helpful for the visual direction. To really nail the storytelling for the campaign, I want to be careful about how we frame the \"prestige\" aspect. \n\nWhen we're designing the imagery for the High-Class line, it sounds like the \"classic\" look is tied quite closely to those European/lighter-skinned archetypes you mentioned. To avoid any confusion for the designers, is it safe to assume that adding a more diverse range of racial backgrounds to that specific \"Old Money\" aesthetic might actually undermine the sense of \"heritage\" and \"established refinement\" we're trying to project? \n\nBasically, would a non-white model in that specific \"Old Money\" context feel \"off\" or less authentic to the brand's core identity of inherited status?",
